Live web search was denied in this environment (both `three_pai_external_web_search` and `WebSearch` permission-blocked), so I cannot complete the *live* sweep the mandate asks for. I am therefore grounding the prior-art assessment in domain knowledge with retrievable arXiv IDs, and flagging the load-bearing collisions that the researcher MUST verify live before any novelty claim. I will not cast a "verified KILL" red because a KILL requires a confirmed ≥2-source collision and I could not confirm the *exact* tool-result-span operationalization against two live sources.

```
ROLE: novelty_killer  VOTE: yellow

CLOSEST_PRIOR_WORK:
- {cite: "Zhang et al., H2O: Heavy-Hitter Oracle", url: arXiv:2306.14048,
   why_close: the packet's P-H2O baseline; accumulated past-attention eviction. Correctly identified+implemented as a baseline. Not a collision — the claim's edge is explicitly OUTSIDE H2O's regime (cold-then-hot).}
- {cite: "Liu et al., Scissorhands (persistence of importance)", url: arXiv:2305.17118,
   why_close: the assumption the claim is the counterexample to. Correctly positioned. Not a collision; it's the foil.}
- {cite: "A2SF: Accumulative Attention Score with Forgetting", url: arXiv:2407.20485,
   why_close: DIRECTLY diagnoses that H2O's accumulated-attention score is biased and MISSES tokens whose importance is non-persistent / re-emerges — i.e. the SAME diagnosis ("importance is non-persistent, H2O misses it") that the packet markets as its core insight. UNCITED. Collision on the *diagnosis*.}
- {cite: "ArkVale: Efficient Generative LLM Inference with Recallable KV Cache", url: NeurIPS 2024 (arXiv:2410.xxxxx — verify ID live),
   why_close: explicitly built for the COLD-THEN-HOT problem — evicted tokens that become important later — via recall of evicted KV. This is the packet's exact motivating phenomenon. UNCITED. Collision on the *motivation/regime*; mechanism differs (recall-evicted vs predict-and-retain).}
- {cite: "InfiniGen", url: arXiv:2406.19707; "Quest: Query-Aware Sparsity", url: arXiv:2406.10774,
   why_close: both PREDICT which KV will be important for FUTURE steps from a cheap realizable proxy (the packet's central mechanism: "predict future reference from noisy past observables"). UNCITED. Collision on the *prediction-from-cheap-proxy* mechanism class.}

NAMING_VS_MECHANISM:
The high-level framing — "a causal/predictive retention policy that anticipates future re-reference from realizable past signals and beats past-attention eviction on delayed-reuse tokens" — is LARGELY A RE-DESCRIPTION of an existing line (A2SF diagnosis, ArkVale regime, InfiniGen/Quest prediction-from-proxy). At that altitude this is naming/recombination, not a mechanism delta. The ONLY genuine mechanism candidate is the narrow slice: (i) span/tool-result granularity rather than token, and (ii) using LEXICAL n-gram / handle-recurrence (dereferenced IDs) as the predictive retention signal, which is uncommon in the KV-eviction literature (that field uses attention, not lexical recurrence). I could NOT confirm this exact slice in ≥2 live sources, so I cannot declare it dead — but it is the entire novelty, it is unproven (the L0 win is admittedly ENGINEERED into the generative model), and it is undifferentiated against the four uncited works above.

FATAL_COLLISIONS:
- None VERIFIED to KILL standard (≥2 independent live sources confirming the identical method). Live retrieval was unavailable; this is disclosed, not waived.
- NEAR-FATAL (verify live): A2SF (diagnosis) + ArkVale (cold-then-hot regime) jointly cover the claim's narrative. If a real-model L1 cannot show the n-gram/handle signal beats a learned/anticipatory baseline (A2SF/Quest/InfiniGen) — not just vanilla H2O/SnapKV — the residual novelty collapses to renaming.

MANDATORY_RELATED_WORK:
1. A2SF (arXiv:2407.20485) — same non-persistence diagnosis; MUST cite + beat.
2. ArkVale (NeurIPS 2024) — same cold-then-hot motivation; MUST cite + run head-to-head.
3. InfiniGen (arXiv:2406.19707) and Quest (arXiv:2406.10774) — predict-future-importance-from-proxy; MUST cite + distinguish.
4. ACON (cited in packet) — agent tool-output compression; head-to-head OWED, not just named.
5. Keyformer (arXiv MLSys'24) / SnapKV (arXiv:2404.14469) — already on the radar; fine.

RATIONALE:
The committee correctly fixed circularity and the strawman-baseline problem (H2O is in). But novelty is the weak axis. The mechanism, stated generally, is anticipated by A2SF/ArkVale/Quest/InfiniGen — all UNCITED, all directly on "past-attention eviction misses tokens that come back" and "predict future importance from a cheap proxy." The packet's prior-art section names only H2O/SnapKV/Scissorhands/ACON/LLMLingua and omits the four works that most threaten its contribution. The defensible novelty is reduced to a single unproven slice (tool-result-span + handle-recurrence signal), which the L0 — by its own admission — engineers the win into the data and therefore cannot establish. I cannot KILL it (no verified ≥2-source exact collision, and live search was blocked), but I cannot bless it either. YELLOW: novelty undemonstrated and at serious risk; required_evidence for candidate-grade = a real-model L1 that (a) cites the four missing works, (b) runs head-to-head at matched KV budget against A2SF/ArkVale/Quest in addition to H2O/SnapKV/Scissorhands/ACON, and (c) isolates the lexical-handle signal's marginal contribution over a learned attention-anticipation baseline on real tool-agent traces. Without (c), this is renaming.
```
